<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining! I Need an Umbrella.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize familiar sounds from audio sources. Interactive: Engage in a role-play based on a listened scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize weather descriptions from audio sources (e.g., "It's sunny."). Can engage in a role-play to show the meaning of simple information about the weather in everyday life (e.g., "What is that?" "An umbrella!" "Why do you need an umbrella?" "It is raining!"). Can get the gist of a simple role-play performed by peers (e.g., after watching a short role-play, they can answer, "Why is Mario sad? "It's raining!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher plays various weather sounds (e.g., rain, wind, sun with birds chirping). Students close their eyes and listen. After each sound, the teacher asks, "What do you hear?" and displays corresponding large picture flashcards (rain, wind, sun). The teacher also shows an umbrella and asks, "What is this? Why do I need it?" activating prior knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary using flashcards and actions: "It's raining!" (mimics rain falling), "It's sunny!" (smiles, points to sun flashcard), "It's windy!" (sways, mimics wind blowing). The teacher then introduces "umbrella," "coat," "boots" with realia or flashcards. Focus is placed on phonemes /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining." Teacher models blending: "/r-eɪn/ - rain," "/ˈr-eɪn-i/ - rainy." Teacher also models feelings: "When it's raining, I feel cold/sad. When it's sunny, I feel happy!" using contractions "It's" and "I'm".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and simple questions to check understanding.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of rain) "Is it sunny or rainy?" (Students point or say "Rainy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a child with an umbrella in the rain) "Why does she need an umbrella?" (Students gesture "rain" or say "Rain!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of the sun) "How do you feel when it's sunny?" (Students might say "Happy!" or show a happy face).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio story or dialogue titled "Leo's Rainy Day" featuring characters discussing the weather and their feelings. The audio should explicitly use "It's raining," "I need an umbrella," "I'm sad," "I'm happy," "How do you feel?" and words like "sun," "rain," "cloud," "wind," "umbrella," "coat," "boots." Visuals (pictures of characters and weather) accompany the audio. Register is informal, dialogue format.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner understands simple, familiar words and very basic phrases by listening attentively to short, spoken messages or stories related to daily routines and weather descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen and hold up the correct weather flashcard (sun, cloud, rain, wind) each time the weather is mentioned.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Specific Information Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and point to the character who feels "happy" or "sad" as described in the audio. They also point to the item needed (umbrella, coat, boots) when mentioned.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual):</b> The teacher plays short snippets of the audio. After each snippet, the teacher shows a visual (e.g., "It's raining!" with a picture of a sunny day). Students respond "True" or "False" by showing a thumbs up/down, or by pointing to a "happy" or "sad" face icon if the picture matches the audio.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Learners practice listening for and identifying weather descriptions, feelings, and associated objects. This includes grammar (contractions "It's," "I'm," WH-questions "How do you feel?"), vocabulary (sun, rain, cloud, wind, umbrella, coat, boots, happy, sad), and pronunciation (/s/ in sunny, /r/ in raining, phonemic blending).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides worksheets with columns of spoken phrases (e.g., "It's sunny," "It's raining," "I need an umbrella") and pictures. The teacher reads each phrase aloud, and students listen and draw a line to match the phrase to the correct picture. The teacher repeats the phrases to ensure clarity, emphasizing /s/ and /r/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Choose (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher plays audio clips where a speaker describes the weather or a feeling, but pauses before the key word (e.g., "It's... [beep]"). Students listen and choose the correct word from a set of two spoken options provided by the teacher (e.g., "sunny" or "rainy"). Or, the teacher says a sentence with a feeling, "I'm feeling..." and students choose a flashcard showing "happy" or "sad."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Act (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a set of weather flashcards and feeling cards. Student A closes their eyes. Student B picks a weather card (e.g., "It's raining!") and makes the sound. Student A listens and opens eyes to pick the corresponding weather and feeling card (e.g., "rainy" and "sad") and says, "It's raining. I'm sad." Student B then confirms, "Yes, that's right!" or "No, try again!" They swap roles, focusing on clear pronunciation and listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Role-Play "Weather Watchers." Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of picture cards depicting various weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and objects (umbrella, coat, boots) as well as feeling cards (happy, sad, cold, wet).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student (the "Weather Reporter") secretly chooses a weather card and makes the corresponding weather sound (e.g., "Pitter-patter" for rain, "Whoosh" for wind).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The other students (the "Listeners") listen to the sound. Based on the sound, one listener says, "It's [raining/sunny/windy]!" and then another listener asks, "How do you feel?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The first student to correctly identify the weather and respond with an appropriate feeling (e.g., "I'm cold!" or "I'm happy!") and/or need (e.g., "I need an umbrella!") gets to be the next Weather Reporter.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing listening skills and encouraging the use of polite phrases like "I need an umbrella, please." and "Thank you."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly simulates reacting to different weather conditions, emphasizing the theme "It's Raining! I Need an Umbrella." and the scenario of observing "This Beautiful Day" (or not-so-beautiful day).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Weather Watchers" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes each student's ability to listen to the weather sound/description and provide an appropriate verbal response using target language during the role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes listened weather description (e.g., "It's raining!"). (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., "umbrella," "coat," "happy," "sad," "sunny," "rainy"). (Few/Some/Many)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple sentences/phrases ("I need an umbrella," "I'm sad," "It's raining"). (Emerging/Developing/Consistent)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages collaboratively in the role-play. (Limited/Participates/Actively participates)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /s/ and /r/ sounds in target words is intelligible. (Needs practice/Good/Excellent)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a short paper with two questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "Today, I can hear and understand the word: (Circle one) <b>rain</b> / <b>sun</b> / <b>wind</b>"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "I feel (draw a happy face 😊 or a sad face ☹️) about my listening today."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral or written depending on time):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new weather words did you hear and understand today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How do you feel when it's sunny, and how do you feel when it's raining?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in developing listening skills for this lesson? (e.g., audio stories, sound effects, visual aids).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What vocabulary or grammar points were challenging for students to hear and recognize?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) to build upon the listening foundation, especially for students struggling with phonemic awareness and initial sound recognition?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job listening today, everyone! Next time, we will *read* about the weather and see pictures of 'This Beautiful Day' in a story. We will use the same words like 'sunny' and 'rainy' to understand what we read."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>